Vendored MD5 (public domain)
============================

Source:   Alexander Peslyak (Solar Designer), "OpenSSL-compatible MD5", 2001.
          http://openwall.info/wiki/people/solar/software/public-domain-source-code/md5
Licence:  public domain (fallback: permissive, "redistribution and use ... are permitted"); see the header of md5.c.
Used by:  the archive download (M3), to detect a truncated or corrupted download.

Upstream: md5/md5.c and md5/md5.h from https://github.com/openwall/popa3d (the same file the homepage links).
md5.c equals upstream except for one marked "OpenShop" line: the x86 unaligned-read shortcut in SET() is skipped when
OPENSHOP_MD5_PORTABLE is defined. md5.h only gained extern "C" guards for C++ (marked "OpenShop").
On x86 upstream reads words directly; on the big-endian Wii it uses the portable byte-wise SET(). Makefile.pc always defines
OPENSHOP_MD5_PORTABLE=1, so the PC app and every host test run the SAME byte-wise code as the Wii (and UBSan does not flag the
unaligned word reads that arbitrary HTTP slices would cause; Increment 8's installer_test found that). The MD5 itself is
checked by tests/install_test.cpp (RFC 1321 vectors, chunked versus one-shot hashing, 1..n-byte slices) and installer_test.cpp.

MD5 here detects corruption only. Archives come over plain http, so it says nothing about tampering.
